content.text:
I now have a complete, precise picture. Here is the ground-truth report.

## 1. The `Node` model (Proxmox host) — `prisma/schema.prisma:1131-1157`

```
/// A Proxmox hypervisor the platform can provision onto. The primary node
/// represents this host; additional nodes are onboarded via scripts/onboard-node.sh.
model Node {
  id            String     @id @default(cuid())
  name          String     @unique
  pveNode       String // Proxmox node name (e.g. ns5002569)
  apiUrl        String // https://host:8006
  tokenId       String
  tokenSecret   String
  publicIp      String
  bridge        String     @default("vmbr1")
  subnetPrefix  String     @default("10.20.0.") // /24 NAT subnet prefix
  ipStart       Int        @default(100)
  ipEnd         Int        @default(250)
  sshPortStart  Int        @default(20000)
  sshPortEnd    Int        @default(29999)
  natChain      String     @default("NXEON_FWD")
  storageNvme   String     @default("vmdata")
  storageHdd    String     @default("vmdata-hdd")
  capacityRamMB Int        @default(56000)
  isPrimary     Boolean    @default(false)
  enabled       Boolean    @default(true)
  status        NodeStatus @default(ONLINE)
  lastSeenAt    DateTime?
  createdAt     DateTime   @default(now())
  servers       Server[]
}
```
`enum NodeStatus { ONLINE OFFLINE MAINTENANCE }` — `prisma/schema.prisma:1125-1129`.

- **No `provider` field, no `region`/`location`/`country`/`datacenter` field anywhere on `Node`.** Identity is only `name` (unique, human label, e.g. `"primary"`) and `pveNode` (the actual Proxmox node name, e.g. `ns5002569`).
- **FK**: `Server.nodeId String?` + `Server.node Node? @relation(fields: [nodeId], references: [id])` — `prisma/schema.prisma:631-632`. Nullable, `onDelete` unspecified (default restrict-ish behavior in Prisma without explicit action).
- **Schema already supports multiple `Node` rows** — the doc comment literally says "additional nodes are onboarded via `scripts/onboard-node.sh`", and every query (`scheduleNode`, `nodeTargets`, `proxmoxNodeUtil`) does `findMany`, never assumes one row.
- **Live DB check** (queried directly via psql against `DATABASE_URL` in `.env`): only **one** `Node` row exists today — `name="primary"`, `pveNode="ns5002569"`, `publicIp="51.79.162.96"`, `isPrimary=true`, `status=ONLINE`. 15 servers have `nodeId` set to it; 4 servers have `nodeId = NULL` (these are the game-order rows the comment at `lib/node-capacity.ts:329-338` describes as historically unscheduled).
- Separately there's a parallel `HostingNode` model (`prisma/schema.prisma:1399-1434`) for shared-hosting boxes — same "one node type" pattern, own capacity fields (`maxAccounts`, `diskTotalGB`), and (unlike `Node`) it has **SSH** fields: `sshHost`, `sshPort` (default 22), `rootPasswordEnc`. Also only 1 row exists today (`hosting-1`, `51.79.226.201`).

## 2. `lib/proxmox.ts` (921 lines, read in full)

- Module-level singleton, built from env vars, `lib/proxmox.ts:6-8`:
  ```
  const BASE = process.env.PROXMOX_API_URL ?? "https://127.0.0.1:8006";
  const NODE = process.env.PROXMOX_NODE ?? "";
  const TOKEN = `PVEAPIToken=${process.env.PROXMOX_TOKEN_ID}=${process.env.PROXMOX_TOKEN_SECRET}`;
  ```
- `export const pve = { get, post, put, del }` (`lib/proxmox.ts:58-63`) is bound to that single env-derived `BASE`/`TOKEN` — this is "the primary node" client, hardcoded to one set of credentials via env vars.
- `export const node = NODE;` (`lib/proxmox.ts:65`) — the bare env `PROXMOX_NODE` string.
- **`NodeConn` already takes arbitrary per-node connection details** (`lib/proxmox.ts:68-73`):
  ```
  export type NodeConn = { apiUrl: string; pveNode: string; tokenId: string; tokenSecret: string };
  ```
  This is NOT hardcoded — it's literally shaped 1:1 to the `Node` Prisma model's `apiUrl`/`pveNode`/`tokenId`/`tokenSecret` fields.
- **`pveFor(conn: NodeConn): PveClient`** (`lib/proxmox.ts:87-117`) builds a fully independent client (own token, own base URL, own `waitForTask`/`nextVmid`/`vmStatus` bound to `conn.pveNode`) — comment at line 86: `"Use in the worker for multi-node."` Multi-node dispatch already works mechanically.
- However, a large number of standalone helper functions in this same file still hardcode the single env-derived `pve`/`NODE` singleton rather than taking a `PveClient`/`NodeConn`: `waitForTask` (119-132, uses module `NODE`), `nextVmid` (263-266), `vmStatus` (476-478), `nodeStatus` (480-482), `listVms` (484-486), `nodeRrd` (497-499), `listSnapshots` (676-679), `listBackups` (681-684), `storageStatus` (919-921). These will always talk to "the primary node" regardless of which node a given server actually lives on, unless callers route through `pveFor`/`PveClient`/`TaskClient` variants instead (which do exist in parallel — e.g. `vmRrd` at 491-495, `listBackupsOn` at 792-798, `archivedVmids` at 464-474, `waitForTaskProgress`/`TaskClient` at 138-261 — all node-conn-aware).

## 3. Node selection during provisioning

**`lib/node-capacity.ts:340-361` — `scheduleNode(planRamMB)`** is the capacity-based picker, already multi-node aware:
```js
export async function scheduleNode(planRamMB: number) {
  const nodes = await db.node.findMany({ where: { enabled: true, status: "ONLINE" } });
  if (nodes.length === 0) return null;
  const loads = await Promise.all(nodes.map(async (n) => {
    const agg = await db.server.findMany({ where: { nodeId: n.id, status: { notIn: ["DELETING"] } }, select: { plan: { select: { ramMB: true } } } });
    const used = agg.reduce((s, r) => s + r.plan.ramMB, 0);
    const projectedPct = n.capacityRamMB > 0 ? ((used + planRamMB) / n.capacityRamMB) * 100 : 100;
    return { node: n, free: n.capacityRamMB - used, projectedPct };
  }));
  const { critPct } = await nodeThresholds();
  const roomy = loads.filter((l) => l.projectedPct < critPct);
  const pool = roomy.length > 0 ? roomy : loads;
  pool.sort((a, b) => b.free - a.free);
  return pool[0].node;
}
```
Bin-packs on RAM headroom only (no CPU/disk/provider/region weighting), picks the single most-free-RAM node under the CRITICAL threshold (falls back to least-bad if all are critical). It is the **single shared implementation** — comment at `lib/node-capacity.ts:328-338` documents it replaced two duplicate copies and fixed `game-order.ts` never scheduling at all.

Call sites: `lib/order.ts:666` (VPS orders), `lib/game-order.ts:348`, `lib/actions-nxcloud.ts:107`, `lib/custom-plan.ts:119`.

`lib/order.ts:660-668` — explicit customer node choice overrides the scheduler:
```js
let nodeId: string | null = null;
if (intent.nodeId) {
  const n = await db.node.findFirst({ where: { id: intent.nodeId, enabled: true } });
  if (!n) throw new OrderError("Selected location is not available.");
  nodeId = n.id;
} else {
  const n = await scheduleNode(plan.ramMB);
  nodeId = n?.id ?? null;
}
```
`intent.nodeId` comes straight from a deploy-wizard form field (`lib/order.ts:250`, `intentFromFormData`).

**Customer-facing UI already exists**: `components/customer/DeployWizard.tsx:50` — `type NodeOpt = { id: string; name: string; publicIp: string }`; `:319` — `const multiNode = nodes.length > 1;` (only shown once a 2nd node exists); `:1063-1076` — a "Choose a location" step that renders `n.name` + `n.publicIp` as the only identifying info per node (no region/provider shown, because none exists on the model). Source of `nodes` prop: `app/dashboard/deploy/page.tsx:57` — `db.node.findMany({ where: { enabled: true }, select: { id, name, publicIp } })`.

`worker/index.ts:109-118` — `nodeFor(server)` resolves the actual Proxmox client per server at execution time (already multi-node-safe at the worker layer):
```js
async function nodeFor(server: any) {
  const n = server.node ?? (await db.node.findFirst({ where: { isPrimary: true } })) ?? (await db.node.findFirst({ where: { enabled: true } }));
  if (!n) { /* env-fallback singleton client */ }
  return { conn: pveFor({ apiUrl: n.apiUrl, pveNode: n.pveNode, tokenId: n.tokenId, tokenSecret: n.tokenSecret }), row: n };
}
```
Used at 11 call sites in `worker/index.ts` (lines 478, 850, 895, 998, 1014, 1190, 1288, 1334, 1401, 1498).

Admin CRUD for nodes: `lib/actions-nodes.ts` (302 lines) — `addNode`, `updateNode`, `setNodeEnabled`, `setNodeStatus`, `testNode`, `deleteNode`, `suggestNodeDefaults`. None of these fields include provider/region.

`scripts/onboard-node.sh` — the operational runbook for bringing up node N. Its own header (lines 28-49) is explicit about a real limitation relevant to a Hetzner box: **each node NATs its own private `/24` behind its own public IP; VMs on different physical hosts cannot reach each other over the private network** — cross-host east-west needs a separate overlay (WireGuard/VXLAN), explicitly out of scope of that script.

## 4. IP pool model — `DedicatedIp` (`prisma/schema.prisma:1070-1086`)

```
enum DedicatedIpStatus { FREE ASSIGNED PENDING RELEASING }
enum DedicatedIpPurpose { CUSTOMER INTERNAL }

/// A public IPv4 (OVH failover/additional IP) that can be assigned to one VM.
/// Synced from the OVH account; each has an OVH Virtual MAC when in use.
model DedicatedIp {
  id           String             @id @default(cuid())
  address      String             @unique
  ovhBlock     String // the OVH IP block/route this belongs to
  virtualMac   String?
  status       DedicatedIpStatus  @default(FREE)
  serverId     String?            @unique
  server       Server?            @relation(fields: [serverId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  reverse      String?
  purpose      DedicatedIpPurpose @default(CUSTOMER)
  purposeLabel String?
  createdAt    DateTime           @default(now())
}
```
**Flat and global — no `nodeId`, no `region`, no provider field at all.** It is implicitly bound to exactly one OVH dedicated server via an env var, not a DB column: `lib/dedicated-ip.ts:41` — `const SERVER = process.env.OVH_SERVER_NAME ?? "ns5002569.ip-51-79-162.net";`. Every OVH call in that file (`syncDedicatedIpPool`, `findVirtualMacForIp`, `allocateDedicatedIp`, `releaseDedicatedIp`) hardcodes that same single `SERVER` constant — there is currently no way to have a second pool of dedicated IPs scoped to a different physical server/provider. `ovhGateway()` (`lib/dedicated-ip.ts:73-77`) also derives the failover gateway from a single `PUBLIC_IP` env var (`…/24`'s `.254`), which is an OVH-specific convention (Hetzner failover IPs work differently).

Live DB counts: 1 FREE/CUSTOMER, 5 ASSIGNED/CUSTOMER, 3 ASSIGNED/INTERNAL — 9 rows total, all against the one OVH server.

## 5. `lib/ovh.ts` usage surface (92 lines, read in full)

Exports: `ovhConfigured()`, `ovh(method, path, body)` (signed AK/AS/CK client, endpoint keyed by `OVH_ENDPOINT` env var — `ovh-eu`/`ovh-ca`/`ovh-us`), and `ovhApi` convenience wrappers (`me`, `servers`, `server`, `ips`, `ipInfo`, `virtualMacs`, `createVirtualMac`, `setReverse`).

Grep for every importer of `./ovh` (whole repo):
- `app/admin/ips/page.tsx`
- `worker/index.ts`
- `scripts/set-mail-ptr.ts`
- `lib/dedicated-ip.ts`

Additionally, `lib/dedicated-ip.ts` is itself imported far more widely (i.e. every one of these transitively assumes an OVH pool even if they don't import `ovh.ts` directly): `app/dashboard/page.tsx`, `app/dashboard/billing/page.tsx`, `app/dashboard/deploy/page.tsx`, `app/dashboard/servers/[id]/page.tsx`, `app/admin/ips/page.tsx`, `app/admin/settings/page.tsx`, `app/admin/accounting/expenses/page.tsx`, `app/admin/accounting/page.tsx`, `worker/restore.ts`, `worker/index.ts`, `worker/billing.ts`, `test/money-core-guards.test.ts`, `components/admin/settings/DedicatedIpPanel.tsx`, `components/customer/AddDedicatedIp.tsx`, `prisma/seed-blog-domains.ts`, `lib/game-order.ts`, `lib/actions-accounting.ts`, `lib/dedicated-ip-purchase.ts`, `lib/fulfil-checkout.ts`, `lib/actions-payment-method.ts`, `lib/order.ts`, `lib/actions-ovh.ts`, `lib/annual-discount.ts`, `lib/actions.ts`, `lib/actions-dedicated-ip.ts`, `lib/restore-identity.ts`, `lib/actions-settings.ts`.

`lib/actions-ovh.ts` (62 lines) is a thin admin-actions wrapper around the OVH pool/sync flow — another OVH-only call site.

## 6. Existing "region"/"location" concepts elsewhere in the codebase

Grepped the entire schema for `region|location|datacenter` (case-insensitive). Every hit is unrelated to node/server infra:
- `Visit.region String?` (`prisma/schema.prisma:2703`) — visitor geolocation for marketing analytics, parsed from IP via a geo lookup.
- `GeoCache.region String?` (`prisma/schema.prisma:2792`, model at 2788-2795) — an IP→location cache (`country`/`region`/`city`/`lat`/`lng`) populated best-effort by the worker for the analytics dashboard.
- `Business.region String @default("")` (`prisma/schema.prisma:4017`) — a CRM/leads field for manually-entered company records (Phase 1 leads feature), unrelated to hosting infra.

No `Region` enum exists anywhere. **Nothing to reuse** — a region/provider concept for `Node`/`DedicatedIp` would be a genuinely new addition, not a naming collision with an existing convention.

## 7. SSH access to the node vs. the guest VM

- The Proxmox `Node` model has **no** SSH fields at all (no `rootPasswordEnc`, no `sshHost`, no `sshPort` — confirmed by grepping the whole schema for those three field names, `prisma/schema.prisma:564-566` and `:1403-1407` are the only hits, both belonging to `Server` and `HostingNode` respectively, never `Node`). The platform talks to Proxmox hosts exclusively through the Proxmox HTTP API (`apiUrl` + `tokenId`/`tokenSecret`), never direct root SSH.
- `HostingNode` **does** have direct-SSH fields (`sshHost`, `sshPort` default 22, `rootPasswordEnc`) because the shared-hosting control panel provisions/manages that box (nginx vhosts, DBs, cron, services) over SSH — `worker/hosting.ts`, `lib/hosting-fs.ts`, `lib/hosting-db.ts`, `lib/ssh-exec.ts`.
- `lib/ssh-exec.ts` (confirmed by reading it) connects using `SftpServer`/`connectionTarget`/`serverRootPassword` from `lib/sftp.ts` — i.e. it targets a customer's guest VM (`Server` row), not a physical host. The comment at the top of the file (line 8) is explicit: *"the customer's root credentials stay on the server"* (the guest).
- Net: today the app SSHes into (a) individual guest VMs via `Server.rootPasswordEnc`, and (b) the one `HostingNode` box directly via its own dedicated SSH fields. It never SSHes into a Proxmox `Node` (compute host) directly for anything — all Proxmox host operations go through the token-authenticated API client (`pve`/`pveFor`).
